@pablosanco Gracias Pablo.

A los modelos hay que realizarles un proceso que se denomina "decimado", reducir el número de caras de la malla, pero de forma eficiente para que no pierda la forma ni la resolución de la textura. Lo mas complejo es volver a texturizar una vez reducida la malla.

Se puede hacer con Meshlab (programa gratuito), o incluso en CloudCompare (también gratuito).

@djkajimusic Hey! The central planks were reused using Array, which helped with consistency and modeling time. The upper and lower planks needed specific adjustments because they have slight differences in inclination and fit with the metal structure. When I tried to use exactly the same mesh as the central planks, there were slight distortions in the contour and a risk of intersection with the metal, so I did a separate modeling to maintain the correct fit.

Oh, and although the texture is similar, small differences in the mesh help to avoid perfect repetition and make the result more natural, especially at the ends, which are areas where the eye tends to notice more details, so it's kind of a win-win situation here. If all the planks were identical in silhouette, it would be possible to instantiate the same piece, but in this particular bench these structural variations required slightly different topologies.

Thank you for observing the topology and for your interest in the model!

